>>
>> xorlo is but a tiny fraction of what sets apart CLL-Lojban from
>> Now-Lojban.
>
> Anyone who uses something other that CLL-Lojban plus xorlo is not using
> the standard language.=A0 I, for one, probably won't understand them,
> because I have explicitly chosen NOT to learn experimental/proposed
> changes.=A0 I suspect that a lot of others are like me.

A good example is the evolution of {ka}. Nowadays, most proficient=20
speakers would not consider {lo ka ta pelxu} a well-formed expression,=20
even though the CLL accepts it.

There are many more such cases. It would be naive to assume that=20
everyone is still speaking CLL-Lojban.

mu'o mi'e la selpa'i

